SPU: rdch SPU_RdMachStat bit 31 (value 1) returns the context's tracked interrupt-enable state by default (was the constant 1 while running).

Problem: All 4 One (Luminopolis, ~468-499 s) and Into the Nexus stop flipping with the "ig" SPURS lanes parked at
0x890/0xF8A0 on lock line 0x00F92D80/0x00F51680. Those lanes are idle (no queued work, no end marker; analysis of the
2026-11-19 write-watch runs); the first abnormal event is earlier, in the audio job list (SPURS wid 4, Wws_Job, policy
module A4O 0xDAE900 / Nexus 0xD3F400, image 2). Its job-API entries assert that interrupts are off:
`rdch SPU_RdMachStat; andi 1; brnz 0x1714`, and 0x1714 is `stop 0` (8 of the 9 MachStat reads in each PM branch there).
Jobs enter them through bisld (D bit), so on hardware the read is 0; KZ3 patch 1061's default returns 1 whenever the SPU
runs, so the list stops in its trap and never runs again.

Evidence: a4o-wwatch2.app_stdout.txt l.34511/34516: '"" kernel thread 0 wid=4 PM run not returned for 4538 ms', lane 0
img=2 pc=0x01714 status=0x2 (STOPPED_BY_STOP) lr=0x09248, at t~187 s, the same at every report to the end (l.40319,
407 s later). The PPU's sound command ring 0x00FF3B40 (24-byte records, func_005E8738) then fills: the main thread's
first usleep at lr 0x75A160 (func_0075A0B8, allocation loop on that ring) is l.60951, the freeze second, and it is the
only usleep site from there to the end (a4o-7099: lr 0x759428 from t=468). nexus-wwatch2 l.21954/21958: the same trap
(pc 0x01714 status 0x2 lr 0x0BE68) from t~139 s. Earlier: a4o-scr-y2 (2026-11-10) the same trap at 186 s, and runs
a4o-ie-1 (KZ3_SPU_MACHSTAT_IE=1) and a4o-6508-1 had no wid 4 stuck report (porting-status 2026-11-10 04:58-06:17).
The unmerged R&C 6508 (same semantics) was held back by tods-6508-1 "6 restarts, 7 Lua pairs"; since 2026-11-11 the ToD
gate counts those as respawns (pairs = restarts + 1, fps dependent: base runs pass with 7 and 9), so that run is not
evidence against it.

Fix: the read returns ctx->int_enable (spu_machstat.h, spu_machstat_value). That state is already kept where the hardware
changes it: the E (0x40000, set) and D (0x80000, clear) bits of a taken bi/bisl/bisled/iret/biz/binz/bihz/bihnz in the
lift and the interpreter, cleared by interrupt entry, clear at context start. KZ3_SPU_MACHSTAT_IE=0 restores the old
value for A/B runs only. KZ3_SPU_MACHSTAT_TRACE=1: first read per (image, pc), reads with ie=0, and "[machstat] differs"
for every read whose value the old rule would have changed (tools/tod-regression.sh fails on that line).

Semantics: CBEA v1.02 s.9.8 (SPU_RdMachStat: bit 31 interrupt-enable status, bit 30 isolation, rest 0; the bi-family
E/D bits set/clear the interrupt enable, interrupt entry disables) [src:cbea]; RPCS3's SPU thread returns its
interrupts-enabled flag in bit 0 and the isolation state in bit 1 (read 2026-11-10 for 6508, nothing copied) [src:rpcs3-sputhread].
Host tests (port/rc/tests/run_spu_machstat_test.sh, exits 0 only when both negative controls fail): (1) the real runtime,
port/rc/tests/test_spu_machstat_channel.c linked with this tree's spu_channels.c/spu_interp.c/spu_drain.c (spu_rdch over
every run state, the interpreter running the WWS assert behind a bisld and the AddJob save/restore, interrupt entry and
irete): 47 pass; KZ3_SPU_MACHSTAT_IE=0: 11 fail. (2) the model runtime/spu/tests/test_spu_machstat.c: 84 pass, -DNO_7105:
7 fail. Upstream's runtime/spu/tests/test_spu_machine_status.c tests the same channel but does not build on this tree
(KZ3 patch 1017 made g_spu_trampoline_fn a ctx-> field; its global `ctx` is a struct). RPCS3 reference runs a4o-rpcs3-lumi and nexus-rpcs3-newgame pass these points. Source: SPU notes,
2026-11-19. Supersedes the unmerged 6508.

KZ3 counter-evidence (Killzone 3 the RE notes "Patch 1061" item 2 and the upstream fix list
#289): with this same default KZ3's level load hangs (SPU pass 47, runs spu-47-a1/a2/x2/x3): its WWS AddJob (job_0100d800
0x11640: `rdch RdMachStat; andi 1; bid; ...; bihze`) reads int_enable 0 where hardware has 1, so it stops re-enabling
interrupts and the lane's SN handler never runs again; KZ3 placed the loss between its host-side policy-module entry
(spu_run_policy_module, spurs_policy.c:332, which sets no int_enable) and the job. Why R&C differs (static, 2026-11-19,
SPU images of all 12 R&C titles from work/spu/*/policy,jobmods,images):
- Hardware IE on entry: an LV2 SPU thread starts at its image entry with NPC bit 0 = 0, so IE 0 (CBEA SPU_NPC bit 31 =
  interrupt enable at start; RPCS3's SPU thread init clears its interrupts flag and takes IE from npc bit 0 at start, its
  sys_spu group start stores the entry unmodified, read via raw URL, nothing copied [src:rpcs3-sputhread]); the port's
  spu_context_init gives 0: same. The SPURS kernel that enters a policy module is firmware code (libsre) we cannot read
  here, and RPCS3's HLE kernel (cellSpursSpu.cpp) never touches IE, so the PM-entry IE is not recovered: ASSUMPTION 0
  (the port: 0 on a fresh PM context, else the state the PM left at its exit, which is after a bid at A4O 0x2FFC).
- That PM-entry value reaches no R&C read. Every R&C WWS policy module (ToD, QfB, ACiT, the collection, DLHD, A4O, FFA,
  Nexus) sets IE itself: `bra 0xA2C` vector at LS 0, irete at 0xAEC, bie/bid windows (A4O 0x2594/0x259C, 0x2FDC/0x2FE4),
  and it enters every job by `bisle $0,$5` (A4O 0x35EC, ToD 0x30A4, ACiT 0x3254, rc1 0x32AC: E bit, IE 1, as on
  hardware). The lift applies the E/D bits on that bisl before the call (spu_lifter.py `_ied`), the interpreter too
  (case 2 of the host test). The A4O-family PMs' 9 MachStat reads all assert IE 0 (8 job-API entries behind bisld ->
  0x1714, the interrupt handler at 0x25D4 -> 0x1790); the older PMs read it nowhere.
- KZ3-shape save/restore reads in R&C WWS jobs run with IE 1 from the bisle, so the old rule and this one agree there:
  ToD/QfB jobmods (`rdch; andi 1; ...; bid`, 6 sites) and the collection/DLHD/ToD/QfB jobmods' unoptimised helper (`rdch;
  ori; ... andi 1`, 24 sites). No host path in this tree enters a WWS job without the PM's bisle (spurs_job.c's HLE job
  chain and spu_run_lifted_job*/spu_run_interp_job are SPU-thread/HLE entries that start at IE 0).
What still risks it: (a) the ACiT/Nexus/FFA/A4O EDGE inflate ELF (A4O elf_00d7ad80: 0x3874, 0x3FDC, `rdch; ...; bid;
...; bihze/binze`) is not a WWS job; the port enters it with IE 0, and the IE its real entry path gives it is not
recovered (ASSUMPTION 0). If it is 1 on hardware, its exit no longer re-enables interrupts here; it plants no LS-0
vector or iret, so no handler depends on it, but it is the KZ3 shape. (b) The unrecovered SPURS-kernel IE at PM entry,
if a title reads it before its own bie/bid (no R&C PM does). Both show as "[machstat] differs" with
KZ3_SPU_MACHSTAT_TRACE=1 (the old rule answers 1 where this one answers 0); the sweep gates on that line.
